      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, no worries! I was just trying to make the point that even the most complex sounds are just a sum of simple sine waves, much like how a goblin's chaotic behavior can be broken down into simple, predictable movements if you look closely enough.\n\nTo answer your question about the 440Hz wave: I was explaining that if you wanted to recreate that specific musical note on a computer, you wouldn't need to record the whole thing; you'd just need to tell the computer, \"Play a 440Hz sine wave at 50% volume, and a 660Hz sine wave at 20% volume, and so on.\"\n\nThe beauty of the Fourier Transform is that it acts like a translator for signals. It takes a messy, time-based signal (like a recording of a voice or a radio broadcast) and translates it into a frequency-based map. Instead of asking \"What is happening right now?\", it asks \"What frequencies are making up this signal?\"\n\nThis is incredibly useful in the real world. For example, if you have a noisy signal—like a radio station with static—you can use the Fourier Transform to identify the frequencies of the static and then filter them out",
