      "content": "The Fourier Transform itself doesn't have a magical \"eye\" to distinguish good from bad; it's just a very honest, albeit slightly goblin-like, accountant that lists every single frequency present in the signal, regardless of whether it's useful or just a pile of goblin trash. It doesn't care if a frequency is \"signal\" or \"noise\"; it simply reports, \"Hey, there is 5Hz, 12Hz, 440Hz, and 660Hz.\"\n\nThe distinction happens *after* the transform, when you (or a human engineer) look at that frequency map and decide, \"Okay, that 5Hz and 12Hz is just the goblin static I don't want, but 440Hz is the music I do want.\" You use that knowledge to build a filter—a mathematical gatekeeper—that lets the good frequencies pass through while blocking the bad ones.\n\nThink of it like a goblin market where every vendor sells a specific type of goblin. The Fourier Transform is just the sign at the entrance listing every single goblin type available in the crowd: \"Here are the tiny ones, here are the medium ones, here are the huge ones.\"",
